 ```
 
-Requires Node.js 20+. A Chrome-family browser or Firefox is recommended for session sync; OAuth is available for all platforms.
+The executable is now implemented in Go (language version `1.23.0`). Node.js is only needed for npm packaging and the retained TypeScript regression suite, not to run the CLI. Source installs need Go 1.23 or newer and the pinned dependencies in the Go module cache; builds never fetch dependencies or require native SQLite libraries.
+
+### Go Build
+
+```bash
+go build ./...
+npm run build
+./dist/ft --help
+./dist/fieldtheory --help
+npm run test:go
+npm test
+```
+
+`npm run build` uses the local Go toolchain, disables network/module downloads, and builds both native command names. You can also build them directly with `go build -o dist/ft ./cmd/ft` and `go build -o dist/fieldtheory ./cmd/fieldtheory`. The npm launchers execute these binaries without loading JavaScript; when binaries are absent, they build locally first.
+
+### Migration Scope
+
+The Go CLI owns local paths/configuration, JSONL caches, the schema-version-6 SQLite FTS5 index, search/browsing/statistics, classification, external Claude/Codex invocation, Library/portable-command CRUD, and local agent context. Existing `bookmarks.jsonl`, SQLite databases, preferences, metadata, and Library files remain usable. No cache conversion is necessary: point `FT_DATA_DIR` at the directory containing `bookmarks.jsonl`, then run `ft index`.
+
+X GraphQL/OAuth sync, browser-cookie extraction, media downloading, and macOS app installation/launching are not part of this local-only Go migration. Their historical commands/options remain recognized and return an explicit non-zero explanation rather than invoking the old Node implementation. The sync/app examples below describe the retained integration surface, not network functionality in the Go build.
 
